1027 #edb/ref 17592186048318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1031 "Making Predictions Using Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1033 3753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1034 0.962541274292324 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "369e4b42-eb06-50fe-866b-3c2859ec00eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1031 "Residuals and Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1033 3595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050269 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3b1ff160-959d-503b-acf5-2be698a50baa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428733 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1031 "Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1033 2609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "50060817-b302-5470-841b-1327afaae882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Regression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1033 1057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57cd3867-de9d-5ea0-b2c4-74cd8e11123f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179615 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179615 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/71/7178cf6a642786f249a22cab09178eaa62e63e47a4e3bb28969ca90f4d97db95.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the distances some athletes in a sports club cover during a training session, and the time (in minutes) it takes. Use the trend line to estimate how long it takes a club athlete to run $10$ km." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1900f9ee-5295-5e56-b9d5-2dda8649b212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179615 #edb/ref 1074 "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=10$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/c5/c595c42406bbf7ad4d71b6ceb9914b839f529b26135c467231a38b3cc48223c8.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/28/28f757380ba22cddc96fbd382578a6b8dc9bba258adfec704413047e73d54c93.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](images/85/85754fb62bbee8c597a71ad8fe30e5eabca3ed9c6304b1832e14788b1e809bcd.png)\n\nTherefore, a club athlete can run $10$ kilometers in approximately $45$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179616 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179616 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51ecea6126e2bc887610e5a775f6cc82906c4eb1f569277bbc626d0502bd9efa.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bc871c4-b557-5cff-8ab2-55d9cb6e24fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179616 #edb/ref 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/76/761a2d3608402bb18b3574040556618d5bb0fa2c9bb53ddcb382f3779ae1af0b.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny = mx + b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1.5}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 3 & =0.5(4)+b \\\\ 3 & =2+b \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 5 & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 0.5x & =4 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179617 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179617 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ec/ecce4e60594a69e040328cc8f65300f7ad1ecfe20806d9be08f154fe2beb1246.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a864a65-e753-5bd5-b4fd-b3e95fc5a2de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179617 #edb/ref 1074 "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10x \\\\ 200 & =10x \\\\ \\frac{200}{10} & =x \\\\ 20 & =x \\\\ x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{ mm}.$\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179653 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179654 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1039 "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179655 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1045 "Making Predictions Using Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186179896 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6dfc0cb1-b79c-5ad6-b36f-4f27bb5a1468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/47b77320474a559f69e6ef08b8f6638aac920b05014820ab1b4b82690059c997.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=-0.5x+9.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $x$ when $y=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9a2fa13-4227-5518-b473-4e39aa559784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180065 #edb/ref 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $y=2$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2 & =-0.5x+9 \\\\ 2+0.5x & =9 \\\\ 0.5x & =7 \\\\ x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=2$ we have $x≈14.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=2$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9fed391b00d0159e58b2e760e0c68c5b0be656a440ae898a932bfec64469b5bb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the population of a town recorded at certain times between the years $2000$ and $2020.$\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the year in which the population will be approximately $40\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,56)$ and $(18,48).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f98bb3a-554c-5cbd-87ea-206e88b640d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180068 #edb/ref 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/5b/5b2ab039994e7c2306cad7e30717fb136b5dfc3a028db284a0e95f49b56d580f.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,56)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(18,48).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{48-56}{18-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{16} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,56):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 56 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 56 & =-1+b \\\\ 57 & =b \\\\ b & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+57.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=40$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 40 & =-0.5x+57 \\\\ 0.5x+40 & =57 \\\\ 0.5x & =17 \\\\ x & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the population will be $40\\,000$ around the year $2000+34=2034.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=40$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180358 #edb/ref 1061 194360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c1fdb21-a077-5b08-bb0d-cad183c76182" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180359 #edb/ref 1061 194361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e0ae380d-48eb-5e47-a77c-d9f4180601d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180360 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180360 #edb/ref 1061 194364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e74c6fb4-ea08-5445-b829-46c0e53a1faf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180361 #edb/ref 1061 194365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180361 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "350c6591-826e-553f-b53b-8091df2b9360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180361 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180362 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180362 #edb/ref 1061 194366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180362 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3ec61b0-1600-591b-8322-939ae6586700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180362 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180363 #edb/ref 1061 194369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180363 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "528377e3-e010-5ca3-979e-8aa3d425121f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180363 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180737 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use trend lines to predict values for data that follows a linear trend.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Let's predict the value of $y$ when $x = 5.$\n\n![](images/8a/8ae392764c110d85f913353ddd932fad9d9c7fedde6275c2705cc7f3e92d3844.png)\n\nFirst, we sketch the trend line. If we don't know the trend line's equation, then we simply approximate it.\n\n![](images/2e/2e001c3f1e9dbdc2574913116a0fb73ac70396e97777c56b9d36b683d10d9814.png)\n\nTo make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x = 5$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fb/fb4f4ac52e4e2190b54cf9cf272e720729635c48387e6ac439ffcaf1282a2d7d.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/46/46a2e566d3810362756e6061e3120b7e7140a8a71591a6a1b90fd059b0505ae0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/8d/8db3717a601f2e5b22c507385a04c60531d2ed057adc5372c5dddba174cf703f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x = 5,$ we obtain $y \\approx 10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180737 #edb/ref 1065 5947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180737 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "45f3d201-7eff-5359-a7a1-84d34c8e0e11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180737 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180738 #edb/ref 1064 "If we know the equation of a trend line, we can make more accurate predictions about a given data set. Let's consider an example.\n\nThe pediatric unit of a hospital wants to know the relationship between the age $x$ (in years) and weight $y$ (in kilograms) of their patients. They collected data over several weeks and plotted the diagram given below. \n\n![](images/b6/b63e7db30ad2533058b9e2191f699043784a42b2b3f73525783f720cf84e0fc5.png)\n\nThe hospital finds that the trend line equation is $y = 2x+8.$\n\nNow, suppose the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $3$-year-old child.  To do this, we substitute $x=3$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(3)+8 \\\\  & =6+8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $3$-year-old patient weighs approximately $14\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There is a point on our trend line where $x=3.$ Therefore, the result $y=14$ should be reliable since we can see from the graph that the data follows a linear trend for values of $x$ close to $3.$\n\n- Making predictions inside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **interpolation**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180738 #edb/ref 1065 5948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180738 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6f9baaae-e720-5cb3-a975-cc7467e70fca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180738 #edb/ref 1067 "Make Predictions Using the Trend Line Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180751 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/9c/9cac9d494a95fab39dc7fd71f2d0daf9d474157d453dac61b25ab71da4cc580f.png)\n\nNow suppose that the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $7$-year-old child. They have no data for this age. However, if they assume that the trend continues, they can find the corresponding weight by substituting $x=7$ into the trend line equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(7)+8 \\\\  & =14+8 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $7$-year-old child weighs approximately $22 \\, \\textrm{kg}.$\n\n**Warning:** The point where $x=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, the result $y=22$ *may be unreliable* since we don't know whether values of $x$ close to $7$ follow the same linear trend. The diagram below shows some trends the data *might* follow beyond our initial data range.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea0d5bde6ac6854ea9992cac38f3bf06acb54b0065fff8174d75c89992203b0.png)\n\nMaking predictions outside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **extrapolation**.\n\nWe must be very cautious when extrapolating in real-world situations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180751 #edb/ref 1065 6014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180751 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ece1fab5-157e-5d98-8247-d12f922daf94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180751 #edb/ref 1067 "Extrapolation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266240 #edb/ref 1080 "2028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266240 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266240 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aab51f6-755e-5c2b-a979-93b3e59cb2f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266349 #edb/ref 1080 "$4$ pounds" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "192ae570-5a46-5866-b9fd-ad43da2ee1f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266355 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19b2aac3-d842-5603-b058-bfefeaaf8523" #